## STEP 3 — HYPOTHESES

[HYP] Directory server identity enumeration via public `GET /identity/{id}` endpoint
class: AUTH
asset: https://ds-apip.threema.ch/identity/{identity}
confidence: 75
reasoning: Source code (`fetch-directory.ts`) shows identity lookup uses unauthenticated GET to `identity/{identity}`. 200 for valid IDs, 404 for invalid — a timing/state oracle. Rate-limit (429) is handled client-side, server-side enforcement unclear. While by-design for a directory service, mass enumeration of valid identities enables targeted attacks.
evidence_needed: Confirm 429 rate-limit behavior at scale (does server enforce per-IP throttling?); check if 404 timing differs measurably from valid lookups.
verify_steps: PROBE: curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code} %{time_total}" https://ds-apip.threema.ch/identity/ECHOECHO; curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code} %{time_total}" https://ds-apip.threema.ch/identity/ZZZZZZZZ; send rapid sequential requests to observe 429 trigger point and reset behavior.
impact: Attacker can enumerate valid Threema identities (user lookup). Severity: medium (privacy breach, reconnaissance for targeted phishing/social engineering).
testability: PASSIVE

[HYP] Desktop source: build-time server URLs and public keys baked into client
class: MISCONFIG
asset: threema-desktop vite.config.ts (build-time env vars)
confidence: 55
reasoning: Vite config (`config/vite.config.ts`) embeds `CHAT_SERVER_KEY` (static hex array), `DIRECTORY_SERVER_URL`, `MEDIATOR_SERVER_URL`, `BLOB_SERVER_URL`, `SAFE_SERVER_URL`, `RENDEZVOUS_SERVER_URL`, and `ONPREM_CONFIG_TRUSTED_PUBLIC_KEYS` as build-time constants via `import.meta.env`. While public keys are expected, the static `CHAT_SERVER_KEY` and hardcoded server URLs could enable phishing/downgrade if a malicious build variant were produced or if the key were changed.
evidence_needed: Confirm the key pinning model — is the chat server key the only trust anchor for chat server identity, or is there TLS cert verification too?
verify_steps: PROBE: Review `tls-cert-verifier.ts` and `electron-main.ts` for how CHAT_SERVER_KEY is verified (raw key vs. cert pins); check if non-OnPrem builds have any cert verification for the chat server.
impact: If chat server auth relies solely on a hardcoded public key with no additional TLS cert verification, a man-in-the-middle with a fraudulent cert could impersonate the chat server. Severity: high (MITM of all desktop communications).
testability: PASSIVE

[HYP] Blob server accepts unauthenticated upload to arbitrary blob paths
class: AUTH
asset: https://blob-mirror-{prefix4}.threema.ch/{prefix8}/upload
confidence: 30
reasoning: Source code shows blob upload requires an auth token (`_fetchAuthToken`) fetched from the directory server in OnPrem builds. Non-OnPrem builds return empty auth headers. The blob server returned 403 at root and 400 for valid prefix paths — upload endpoint behavior unconfirmed. Blob server hostname (`blob-mirror-*`) is NOT in scope per scope.yml rules.
evidence_needed: Confirm blob server auth requirements on the `/upload` path; check if 403/400 indicates path-based access control or request format issues.
verify_steps: PROBE: curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}" -X POST https://blob-mirror-0.threema.ch/00/upload -d '{}' -H "Content-Type: application/json"; observe response body for auth challenge indication.
impact: If upload is possible without auth, attacker could store arbitrary files on blob server. Severity: high (storage abuse), but host is out of scope.
testability: PASSIVE
[HYP] Desktop key storage: migration from deprecated path may leave sensitive file with weak permissions
class: MISCONFIG
asset: apps/desktop/src/common/node/key-storage/index.ts (FileSystemKeyStorage)
confidence: 45
reasoning: Key storage file is written with `fileModeInternalObjectIfPosix()` (0600 on POSIX, empty on Windows). On Windows, file permissions are not enforced — the `keystorage.bin` and `keystorage.password.bin` files could be accessible to other processes/users. The password file uses `electron.safeStorage` which has OS-level protection, but the key storage file itself is only protected by the user's password (Argon2id).
evidence_needed: Confirm Windows file permission behavior — does `writeFile` without mode still create accessible files? Check if the password file (`keystorage.password.bin`) provides sufficient protection on Windows.
verify_steps: HUMAN_ONLY: Test on Windows target — check file permissions of `keystorage.bin` and `keystorage.password.bin` after app creation; attempt to read from another process.
impact: On Windows, an attacker with local access could read the encrypted key storage file. Argon2id password provides protection but offline brute-force is possible if password is weak. Severity: medium (local attack, requires access).
testability: HUMAN_ONLY

